kind: perf
upstream: coal PR #858 (open), "Improve the performances of BVH-BVH collision
detection", by coal maintainer lmontaut: its head, d7b7355, merged onto the
pinned devel commit with no conflicts in include/ or src/ (rebased 2026-11-06).
  https://github.com/coal-library/coal/pull/858
  https://github.com/coal-library/coal/commit/d7b73551d8a530d88c03aa1e2d8a722daf1b941a
Drop this patch once #858 is in devel.

Status 2026-11-09: opened 2026-05-13; approved by maintainer jcarpent on
2026-09-22; maintainer jorisv asked the same day for housekeeping only (delete
collision_node.cpp and traversal_recurse.cpp, which the PR empties and drops
from its CMake, and use constexpr now that C++17 is coal's floor); the author
pushed one fix on 2026-10-02 and stopped, and on 2026-11-05 jorisv wrote "This
PR is stall since a lot of time. Can you fix the last issues ?". So the change
itself is reviewed and accepted; only the author's follow-through is missing.
Revisit at each `update`: if it merges, `update` drops this patch; if it is
closed instead, decide again whether 2x on mesh-mesh `collides` is worth
owning these 1680 lines (660 of them are the recursion moving from
traversal_recurse.cpp into traversal_recurse.h, counted twice).

Mesh/mesh collision was 1.5-2x slower than FCL (coal issue #857): the
traversal nodes are virtual, the relative transform is recomputed, and every
pair of triangles runs iterative GJK+EPA. The PR devirtualizes the nodes,
precomputes the relative transform, tests triangle pairs with a closed-form
triangle-triangle specialization, and adds a fast path when contacts are off
and the security margin is 0, which is every `collides` query.

Measured 2026-11-02 (named pairs, boolean collision, us per configuration):
G1 547 -> 256, Talos 914 -> 536, Panda 5 -> 2, with 0 disagreements against
python-fcl.

Two changes of ours on top of the head:
- a plain assert where it calls COAL_ASSERT with only a condition, which does
  not compile
- the fast path only for two triangles that both have area: its closed-form
  test reports any two zero-area triangles as overlapping, however far apart
  (found by fuzz/collision.cpp: two point triangles 0.006 apart, and 10 apart
  too). Those go through the GJK path the leaf always used.

Found by fuzz/collision.cpp and kept as notes, not fixed here:
- Two triangles without area (segments or points) whose closest points touch:
  the mesh distance traversal reports 5.5e-6 where measuring every pair of
  triangles with the same leaf function gives 0. coal devel does the same
  without this patch, so it predates the PR; the harness allows 1e-5 when the
  closest pair has a triangle without area. The input, as fuzz/collision.cpp
  reads it, in hex:
    0a010129000104000000ef0000000000000000002e08000000000000000000000000001b
    000000000000000000000029000104000000ef00000000000000d4d4d4d400002e000000
    0000000000000000000000000000003a00
- Contacts at zero distance: the PR's box test and the leaf test round
  either way when two triangles only touch, so a traversal may report a
  touching pair the closed form doesn't, or miss one. The harness allows
  either when the pair is within 1e-7, and never a miss of a pair that
  truly interpenetrates.

